To solve this, we first find the vectors $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB}.$ We can then use the cross product of these vectors to find a vector $\overrightarrow{n}$ perpendicular to the plane containing $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB}.$**

\overrightarrow{OA} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}, \overrightarrow{OB} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.

\begin{align*}
\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{\hat{i}} & \mathbf{\hat{j}} & \mathbf{\hat{k}} \\
1 & 2 & 3 \\
-3 & -2 & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\
= \mathbf{\hat{i}}(2 \cdot 1 - 3 \cdot (-2)) - \mathbf{\hat{j}}(1 \cdot 1 - 3 \cdot (-3)) + \mathbf{\hat{k}}(1 \cdot (-2) - 2 \cdot (-3)) \\
= \mathbf{\hat{i}}(2 + 6) - \mathbf{\hat{j}}(1 + 9) + \mathbf{\hat{k}}(-2 + 6) \\
= \begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ -10 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

\overrightarrow{n} = \begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ -10 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}

To find the area of the triangle $OAB,$ we take half the magnitude of the cross product of $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB}:$

\begin{align*}
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \left|\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB}\right| \\
= \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{8^2 + (-10)^2 + 4^2} \\
= \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{64 + 100 + 16} \\
= \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{180} \\
= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 2\sqrt{45} \\
= \sqrt{45}
\end{align*}

So, the area of triangle $OAB$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{45}}.$</think> <answer> $\sqrt{45}$ </answer>
